## Proof

Let $S$ be a regular surface parametrized locally by $\mathbf{x}(u,v)$, and let $f:\mathbb{R}^3\to\mathbb{R}^3$ be the homothety $f(\mathbf{p})=c\,\mathbf{p}$ with $c\neq 0$. The image surface $f(S)$ is parametrized by
$$\tilde{\mathbf{x}}(u,v)=c\,\mathbf{x}(u,v).$$

**First fundamental form.** The partial derivatives are $\tilde{\mathbf{x}}_u=c\,\mathbf{x}_u$ and $\tilde{\mathbf{x}}_v=c\,\mathbf{x}_v$, so the coefficients of the first fundamental form satisfy
$$\tilde E = \tilde{\mathbf{x}}_u\cdot\tilde{\mathbf{x}}_u = c^2(\mathbf{x}_u\cdot\mathbf{x}_u)=c^2 E,\quad \tilde F=c^2 F,\quad \tilde G=c^2 G.$$
Thus $\tilde{\mathrm{I}}=c^2\,\mathrm{I}$, and $\tilde E\tilde G-\tilde F^2=c^4(EG-F^2)$.

**Unit normal.** The (un-normalized) normal of $\tilde{\mathbf{x}}$ is $\tilde{\mathbf{x}}_u\times\tilde{\mathbf{x}}_v=c^2(\mathbf{x}_u\times\mathbf{x}_v)$. After normalization the unit normal is unchanged up to sign:
$$\tilde{\mathbf{N}}=\frac{c^2(\mathbf{x}_u\times\mathbf{x}_v)}{|c^2|\,|\mathbf{x}_u\times\mathbf{x}_v|}=\operatorname{sgn}(c^2)\,\mathbf{N}=\mathbf{N}\quad(\text{since }c^2>0).$$
So $\tilde{\mathbf{N}}=\mathbf{N}$.

**Second fundamental form.** The second derivatives are $\tilde{\mathbf{x}}_{uu}=c\,\mathbf{x}_{uu}$, etc. Hence
$$\tilde e=\tilde{\mathbf{N}}\cdot\tilde{\mathbf{x}}_{uu}=\mathbf{N}\cdot(c\,\mathbf{x}_{uu})=c\,e,\quad \tilde f=c\,f,\quad \tilde g=c\,g.$$
Thus $\tilde{\mathrm{II}}=c\,\mathrm{II}$.

**Shape operator / principal curvatures.** The shape operator is $S=-d\mathbf{N}$, expressed in the basis $\{\mathbf{x}_u,\mathbf{x}_v\}$ via the Weingarten equations. Equivalently, the principal curvatures are the eigenvalues of the shape operator, obtained from $\mathrm{II}\,\mathrm{I}^{-1}$. For the scaled surface:
$$\tilde{\mathrm{II}}\,\tilde{\mathrm{I}}^{-1}=(c\,\mathrm{II})(c^{-2}\mathrm{I}^{-1})=\frac{1}{c}\,\mathrm{II}\,\mathrm{I}^{-1}.$$
Therefore the principal curvatures scale as
$$\tilde k_i=\frac{1}{c}\,k_i,\qquad i=1,2.$$

**Gaussian curvature.** Since $K=k_1k_2$,
$$\tilde K=\tilde k_1\,\tilde k_2=\frac{k_1}{c}\cdot\frac{k_2}{c}=\frac{k_1k_2}{c^2}=\frac{K}{c^2}.$$

Equivalently, using the determinant formula $K=\dfrac{eg-f^2}{EG-F^2}$:
$$\tilde K=\frac{\tilde e\,\tilde g-\tilde f^2}{\tilde E\,\tilde G-\tilde F^2}=\frac{c^2(eg-f^2)}{c^4(EG-F^2)}=\frac{eg-f^2}{c^2(EG-F^2)}=\frac{K}{c^2}.$$

**Conclusion.** The Gaussian curvature of the image $f(S)$ under the scaling $f(\mathbf{x})=c\mathbf{x}$ is

$$\boxed{K_{f(S)}=\dfrac{K_S}{c^2}}.$$

